\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  From a conventional BIC to a Rayleigh BIC. (a) A conventional BIC cancels all open radiation. (b) At a Rayleigh anomaly the grazing order has $k_{y,\mathrm  {RA}}=0$ and zero normal flux, but a finite nondecaying amplitude $A_{\mathrm  {RA}}$; the state is not localized. (c) A Rayleigh BIC requires simultaneous cancellation of the grazing amplitude and every remaining open amplitude.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{2}{From a conventional BIC to a Rayleigh BIC. (a) A conventional BIC cancels all open radiation. (b) At a Rayleigh anomaly the grazing order has $k_{y,\mathrm {RA}}=0$ and zero normal flux, but a finite nondecaying amplitude $A_{\mathrm {RA}}$; the state is not localized. (c) A Rayleigh BIC requires simultaneous cancellation of the grazing amplitude and every remaining open amplitude}{figure.1}{}}
